The calculation code in this package was written from scratch from
published scientific sources listed in SOURCES.md. It contains no code
from Swiss Ephemeris, SOFA or ERFA.

This package contains or uses third-party data:

- ΔT since 1973: U.S. Naval Observatory, https://maia.usno.navy.mil/
  (U.S. Government work, public domain).

- ΔT from −720 to 1973: Table S15.2020 from L. V. Morrison,
  F. R. Stephenson, C. Y. Hohenkerk, M. Zawilski, "Addendum 2020 to
  'Measurement of the Earth's rotation: 720 BC to AD 2015'",
  Proc. R. Soc. A 477 (2021), doi:10.1098/rspa.2020.0776, and
  F. R. Stephenson, L. V. Morrison, C. Y. Hohenkerk, "Measurement of the
  Earth's rotation: 720 BC to AD 2015", Proc. R. Soc. A 472 (2016),
  doi:10.1098/rspa.2016.0404. The table is part of the electronic
  supplementary material "Tables S2, S3 and S5 from Addendum 2020 to
  'Measurement of the Earth's rotation: 720 BC to AD 2015'", figshare,
  doi:10.6084/m9.figshare.13885863.v2, licensed under CC BY 4.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Used unmodified;
  converted to Dart constants in lib/src/time/delta_t_data.dart.

- Nutation IAU 2000A and the equation of the equinoxes (tables 5.3a,
  5.3b and 5.2e): IERS Conventions (2010),
  G. Petit and B. Luzum (eds.), IERS Technical Note No. 36, Verlag des
  Bundesamts für Kartographie und Geodäsie, Frankfurt am Main (2010).

- Planetary and lunar ephemerides DE440/DE441 (downloaded, not included):
  R. S. Park, W. M. Folkner, J. G. Williams, D. H. Boggs, "The JPL
  Planetary and Lunar Ephemerides DE440 and DE441", Astron. J. 161:105
  (2021). Courtesy of NASA/JPL-Caltech, Solar System Dynamics,
  https://ssd.jpl.nasa.gov/. Small-body data: JPL Horizons.

- Eclipse test data: "Eclipse Predictions by Fred Espenak, NASA's GSFC"
  (F. Espenak and J. Meeus, Five Millennium Canon of Solar Eclipses and
  of Lunar Eclipses, https://eclipse.gsfc.nasa.gov/). Earth's shadow
  enlargement formulas after the same source.

- Fixed stars: This research has made use of the SIMBAD database, CDS,
  Strasbourg Astronomical Observatory, France (M. Wenger et al.,
  "The SIMBAD astronomical database", A&AS 143, 9, 2000). Source:
  CDS/SIMBAD. Values from F. van Leeuwen, "Validation of the new
  Hipparcos reduction", A&A 474, 653 (2007) and the Washington Double
  Star Catalog, B. D. Mason et al., AJ 122, 3466 (2001); further
  references per value in data/stars/jasne_gwiazdy.csv.

- Star names: IAU Catalog of Star Names, IAU Working Group on Star Names
  (WGSN), https://www.iau.org/public/themes/naming_stars/, licensed under
  CC BY 4.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).
